WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.575 --> 00:00:01.700
DAN BELCHER: Hey, everyone.

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:02.710
I'm Dan Belcher.

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:06.310
And I'm a product manager on
the Google Cloud Platform team.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:07.990
At Google, we
spend a lot of time

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.260
thinking about how we can
make operations easier,

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.540
whether you're running
at Google's scale

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.370
or you're a new startup
looking to launch

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.810
your first application.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.390
Last month, we announced
the beta release

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:21.610
of Google Cloud
Monitoring, which

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.300
is our effort to make
monitoring easier in the cloud.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.340
In this session, we'll
give you a quick overview

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.760
of cloud monitoring
and its capabilities.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.130
Google Cloud Monitoring
gives you insight

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:37.270
to keep your application fast
and available for end users.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:41.230
The Cloud Monitoring console
gives you an at-a-glance view

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.650
into the overall health of
your application, including

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:48.560
your App Engine modules,
groups of resources,

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.110
such as Compute Engine VMs
or Cloud SQL instances,

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.570
endpoint checks,
which measure uptime

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:59.980
of your internet-facing
resources, active incidents

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:06.190
or alerts, KPIs, or
recent changes and events

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.920
within your application.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.530
With no configuration,
Cloud Monitoring

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.920
provides you with rich
dashboards and charts

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.890
that you can use to
identify bottlenecks

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.420
or issues with key metrics.

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:25.610
You can also configure
alerts so that you're

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.150
notified when issues occur.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.970
The system supports a number
of different communication

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:38.020
methods, including email,
PagerDuty, SMS, HipChat,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.130
Campfire, and more.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.010
Cloud Monitoring also
includes rich support

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.120
for popular open
source packages,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.400
such as Cassandra,
MySQL, Nginx, and others.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.190
For instance, you can use the
monitoring plugin for Nginx

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:59.480
to gain access to a
variety of Nginx metrics.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.420
Finally, you can tailor
the monitoring experience

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.920
to your own needs.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.430
With a few clicks, you can
create custom dashboards

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.949
that include metrics
from different components

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:11.580
of your system.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.720
And you can access
advanced charting tools

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.800
to investigate issues.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.340
Cloud Monitoring is available
to all Google Cloud Platform

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.350
users in beta.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.860
You can access Cloud Monitoring
from any of your projects

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.460
by clicking on the Monitoring
link in the left-hand menu

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.490
and then clicking
Dashboards and Alerts.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.130
Cloud Monitoring is
an important part

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:40.090
of how we're making
operations easy at Google.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.750
You can learn more about Cloud
Monitoring today by signing up

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:49.270
for the beta or accessing the
monitoring documentation from

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:52.320
cloud.google.com/monitoring.

